course: dictionary
lesson: 3014
title: Dwell: to live, or to keep thinking
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Dwell» has two main meanings. The first is to live somewhere, and it is often formal or old-fashioned. «Bears dwell in the forest» means bears live in the forest. This is why we talk about city dwellers and cave-dwelling bats.
explain: The second meaning comes with «on» or «upon». To dwell on something means to keep thinking or talking about it, usually when it is sad or bad. «Don't dwell on your mistakes» means stop going over them again and again.
explain: Context tells you which meaning to use. In «it is quite against animal-etiquette to dwell on possible trouble ahead», the words «on possible trouble» point to the second meaning. Etiquette means the rules of polite behaviour, so the sentence says it is bad manners to keep worrying about trouble that might come.
explain: Check by swapping in a meaning. If "keep thinking about" fits, it is the second meaning. If "live in" fits, it is the first.
fact: Dwell is a verb. Its past form can be dwelt or dwelled.
fact: «Dwell» means to live somewhere, and this use is formal or old-fashioned.
fact: «Dwell on» or «dwell upon» means to keep thinking or talking about something, usually something sad or bad.
fact: A dweller is a person or animal who lives in a place, as in city dwellers.
fact: In the rhyme, «frolic» means play and «fret» means worry, so the Rat warns that too much worry can spoil the fun.
exercise: «Bears dwell in the forest.» What does dwell mean here? | *To live somewhere | To sing loudly | To keep thinking about the forest | To run very fast
exercise: Mum says, «Don't dwell on your mistakes.» What does she mean? | *Keep thinking over them again and again | Make the same mistakes again on purpose | Write them down in a long list | Stop thinking over them again and again
exercise: Which word means a person or animal who lives in a place? | etiquette | *dweller | frolicker | fretter
test: What does «etiquette» mean in «it is quite against animal-etiquette to dwell on possible trouble ahead»? | Trouble that is coming | *The rules of polite behaviour | A sort of play or frolic | A kind of cave where bats live
test: In the rhyme, what does «frolic» mean? | Forgetting | Dwelling in a cave | Worry | *Play
exam: Which sentence uses dwell to mean keep thinking about something? | *Please don't dwell on the bad start to your day. | Bears dwell in the cold north. | City dwellers often use buses. | Cave-dwelling bats sleep all day.
